description: |-
  **NEW OPERATION, batch 2026-05-19K** — the master orchestration operation
  for time-based retention + empty-partition cleanup. Per 15-minute fixed-rate
  cycle (`@Scheduled(fixedRate = 15, timeUnit = TimeUnit.MINUTES)` at
  `HousekeepingJobManager.java:25`), the master `runHousekeepingJobs()`
  method:
    1. Acquires the named `housekeepingJob` ShedLock with `lockAtLeastFor =
       "14m"` and `lockAtMostFor = "14m"` (line 26) — exactly 14 minutes,
       providing cluster-wide mutex with razor-thin 60-second slack against
       the 15-minute schedule.
    2. Asserts the lock is held via `LockAssert.assertLocked()` (line 28).
    3. Acquires ONE JDBC `Connection` from `PGConnectionFactory.getConnection()`
       (line 32) — bypasses HikariCP, uses `DriverManager.getConnection`
       directly. The single connection is shared across all five jobs in
       the cycle via try-with-resources.
    4. Iterates the constructor-injected `List<HousekeepingJob>` of FIVE
       beans (lines 33-35): `AlertHousekeepingJob`,
       `SearchFacetsHousekeepingJob`, `DataEntityHousekeepingJob`,
       `ActivityEmptyPartitionsHousekeepingJob`,
       `MessageEmptyPartitionsHousekeepingJob`. Each job's
       `doHousekeeping(connection)` runs synchronously.
    5. Each per-job invocation is wrapped in a try/catch at
       `runHousekeepingJob` (lines 41-47) catching `Exception` (broadest
       checked-or-unchecked). On failure: log at ERROR with the job's
       class name, continue to the NEXT job in the loop. Per-job
       isolation — one failing job does NOT abort the cycle.
    6. SQLException at connection acquisition (line 36) is caught by an
       OUTER try/catch and ABORTS the cycle for all jobs that did not yet
       run — connection-level failure is the only cycle-aborting condition.
  Strict opt-in via `@ConditionalOnProperty(value = "housekeeping.enabled",
  havingValue = "true")` with NO `matchIfMissing` (line 18) — absent key =
  no bean = silent no-op. The shipped `application.yml:166` ships
  `housekeeping.enabled: true` so deployments using the bundled config get
  housekeeping by default; custom application.yml that omits the key
  silently disables the subsystem. The three TTL-driven jobs
  (`AlertHousekeepingJob`, `SearchFacetsHousekeepingJob`,
  `DataEntityHousekeepingJob`) consume `HousekeepingTTLProperties` and
  hard-DELETE rows past the configured cutoff. The two
  `EmptyPartitionsHousekeepingJob` subclasses drop EMPTY past partitions
  only (no TTL coupling). Doc-side: live
  `/configuration-and-deployment/odd-platform#housekeeping` documents the
  master gate + three TTL keys + the 15-minute cadence + the ShedLock
  framing, but enumerates only "three cleanup tasks" — the two
  EmptyPartitions jobs are not surfaced in the docs.
