security_aggregate:
  overall: MEDIUM
  auth_modes_relevant:
  - INTERNAL_ONLY
  authorization_consistency:
    verdict: BYPASSES
    detail: |-
      The orchestrator and all five jobs operate at system scope, deleting
      rows across ALL OWNERS without regard to the ODD ownership model. No
      @PreAuthorize, no programmatic permission check. The bean is
      instantiated regardless of auth.type; cleanup runs under the JVM's
      DB credentials. Intentional — housekeeping is a system-level admin
      operation.
  owner_scoping:
    verdict: BYPASSES
    detail: |-
      DataEntityHousekeepingJob.deleteOwnerships (per the ~25-table
      cascade) removes ALL ownership rows for the soft-deleted entity
      regardless of who the Owners are. The architectural decision is
      consistent with the system-scope intent.
  strengths:
  - Strict opt-in via @ConditionalOnProperty (housekeeping.enabled) —
    deployments customising config without the key silently disable
    (fail-CLOSED on missing config)
  - Per-job isolation — one failing job does NOT abort the cycle;
    other four jobs still run
  - ShedLock-coordinated single-instance execution — multi-replica
    deployments safe by default
  weaknesses:
  - No audit / Micrometer metric / Prometheus counter / structured event
    on the destructive cycle — invisible deletions
  - No dry-run / preview / archival-before-delete mechanism
  - jOOQ operator-precedence bug in AlertHousekeepingJob (manual RESOLVED
    alerts deleted regardless of TTL — known and docs-acknowledged but
    unfixed)
  - `.block()` inside jOOQ transaction in DataEntityHousekeepingJob.deleteFiles
    (S3 hang takes down the entire cycle)
  - lockAtMostFor=14m vs fixedRate=15m razor-thin race surface under
    heavy backlog
performance_aggregate:
  overall: MEDIUM
  hot_paths:
  - |-
    15-min fixed-rate cycle. On busy clusters, a slow
    DataEntityHousekeepingJob cascade (minutes against a large
    soft-deleted backlog) holds the ShedLock for up to 14 minutes and
    may starve other instances.
  - |-
    Sequential single-connection fan-out — five jobs run synchronously on
    one connection; total cycle time = sum of all five jobs' durations.
  throughput_shape:
    verdict: SINGLE_ITEM_ONLY
    detail: |-
      No parallelism across jobs. A slow DataEntityHousekeepingJob delays
      AlertHousekeepingJob + SearchFacetsHousekeepingJob (both fast
      single-table DELETEs).
  scaling_shape:
    verdict: STATEFUL_LOCKED
    detail: |-
      ShedLock-coordinated; only ONE platform instance runs housekeeping
      per 15-minute slot. Horizontal scaling does NOT parallelise
      housekeeping; it provides failover only.
